4. O-ring automation formally validates scarcity migration =E2=80=94 <stron=
g>8.7/10, kernel-level rather than stock-specific</strong>
<p>The January 2026 NBER paper <strong>O-Ring Automation</strong> is unusua=
lly important for our framework. It models production where task qualities =
multiply rather than add. In these systems, smoothly improving automation c=
an trigger discrete bundled adoption =E2=80=94 and automating some tasks ca=
n <strong>increase the value of the remaining human or physical bottleneck =
tasks</strong>.</p>
<p>This is essentially a formal economic foundation for scarcity migration.=
</p>
<p>If output is approximately:</p>
<p><code>Q =3D q1 =C3=97 q2 =C3=97 ... =C3=97 qn</code></p>
<p>then raising nine factors toward infinity does not make the tenth irrele=
vant; it increases the shadow value of improving the tenth.</p>
<p>This gives a general screen:</p>
<ol>
<li>Identify workflows with multiplicative quality/completion constraints.<=
/li>
<li>Estimate which stages AI collapses first.</li>
<li>Recalculate the marginal economic value of the untouched stage.</li>
<li>Buy the provider of that stage <strong>before revenue growth becomes ob=
vious</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p>This is more useful than =E2=80=9CAI exposure=E2=80=9D screens because i=
t predicts winners that have almost no AI narrative at all.</p>

New kernel primitive
<p><code>O_Ring_Gating_Leverage_i =3D downstream value unlocked by marginal=
 improvement in task i / cost of task i</code></p>
<p>Then model its derivative after upstream automation:</p>
<p><code>d(O_Ring_Gating_Leverage_i) / d(AI capability)</code></p>
<p>The largest positive derivatives are the hidden beneficiaries.</p>
